// A DATED, INDEPENDENTLY TRANSCRIBED REALIZATION FIXTURE — NOT LIVE DRIFT DETECTION.
//
// Read this claim precisely, because an earlier revision of this note overstated it and the
// overstatement is the exact failure this repository keeps paying for. What executes here is
//
//     emitter(current) == bytes transcribed from srv1 on 2026-08-19
//
// and NOT
//
//     emitter(current) == host(current)
//
// Nothing reads srv1 at execution time. If the deployed units drift tomorrow while these
// functions stay put, every witness below stays green — so this fixture CANNOT ask "did the host
// change?", and calling the running host an external authority for these bytes would be a claim
// the mechanism does not support. The deployed file is an observed realization, not the authority
// for systemd syntax or for desired deployment state.
//
// What it WAS good for: the expected bytes were obtained independently of the functions under
// test, so a row transcribed from the host and left alone is not a self-capture golden — it
// cannot certify a renderer as correct merely because nobody edited it, which is the change
// detector DESIGN section 5 rejects. A row that still holds its transcription is a sound BASELINE
// for refactoring these renderers: a red means the emitter moved off the last observed deployed
// baseline, which during a migration is exactly the question.
//
// THAT PROPERTY IS NOW TRUE OF ONE ROW OUT OF FIVE, AND THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CLAIM IT FOR ALL OF
// THEM. Independence is not a property of the fixture's design; it is a property of each row's
// EDIT HISTORY, and it is destroyed the moment an author updates an expected literal so that a
// code change goes green. Since the 2026-08-19 transcription (#8496, 8b20599960) that has happened
// three times:
//
//   4b7371d1f6 (#10357, 2026-09-04)  belt-service, serve, tree-sync edited; slice row ADDED
//   75e1cf68b3 (#10446, 2026-09-04)  belt-service edited
//   6a4ca1c5bc (#10590, 2026-09-05)  belt-service and serve edited (user-manager Environment)
//   2026-09-21 (this branch, commitfix)  belt-service: TimeoutStartSec=45min ADDED (per-tick
//                                       watchdog; the 2026-09-21 2.25h wedge froze the cadence
//                                       invisibly. This row is now an EMITTER-REGRESSION PIN like
//                                       the serve and tree-sync rows, and the directive-level
//                                       assertion beside it (watch the Decision, not the bytes)
//                                       pins the watchdog decision itself.
//
// So per row, as of this writing: the belt timer row that held the 2026-08-19 transcription is gone
// with the timer (dogfood intervention #4), so NO row carries an external referent. belt-service, serve
// and tree-sync have each been edited to follow the emitter and are now EMITTER-REGRESSION PINS —
// they still refuse an unintended emission change, which is worth keeping, but they are no longer
// evidence that the emitter agrees with anything outside this tree. The slice row never had a
// transcription at all: it was authored with the emitter in #10357.
//
// The decay is legible in a name: the_tree_sync_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes_PLUS_THE_EXCLUSION
// _WIDENING says out loud that its expectation is host bytes plus an emitter change.
//
// THE OBLIGATION THIS CREATES, stated rather than left to the next reader: an edit to an expected
// literal is a rung change on that row, not a maintenance chore. Either re-transcribe from the
// host and say so with a date, or move the row to a directive-level assertion over the typed
// SystemdUnitFile (gunbc.live_deploy.emit live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file and
// live_deploy_serve_unit_file return one before rendering), which asserts the DECISION — principal,
// slice, working tree, environment derivation — and cannot be satisfied by editing the expectation.
// What is forbidden is what happened here: editing the bytes while a paragraph three screens up
// keeps vouching for their independence. That is DESIGN section 4b rung inflation, and the
// inflated claim was load-bearing — it was cited to justify keeping these rows.
//
// It is needed because nothing else pins these bytes. dag/test/claim/live_deploy/emit.dag
// carries 57 witnesses and not one asserts a unit doc's text; the emitted deploy script is not a
// committed artifact, so git shows no diff; and the generated-artifact drift gate was deleted by
// the 2026-08-15 floor cut (.github/workflows now holds only fleet-converge.yml and witnesses.yml).
//
// Live drift detection is a DIFFERENT mechanism this fixture does not provide: it would have to
// observe the host at run time and would belong with the fleet observation lane, not here.
//
// ── RETIRED WITH THEIR MEMBER, NOT PENDING AN OBSERVATION ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// the_fleet_converge_service_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes and
// the_fleet_converge_timer_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes were transcribed from
// gunbc-srv1-green-fleet-converge.{service,timer}, which existed only on the deleted slot. srv1's
// surviving deployment carries no fleet-converge units — checked on the host 2026-08-19.
//
// THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS RECEIPT GAVE A FALSE REASON, and the correction matters more than the
// rows. It said the members were still in deployment_spec_srv1's step list and would appear at the
// next apply, so the oracle would re-arm then. They are not. gunbc.live_deploy.spec
// deployment_owned_steps lists eight members and FleetConvergeTimerUnit is not among them —
// the annotation on that module's deployment_steps_apply_order records it as RETIRED from
// deployment membership by
// operator directive 2026-08-13, and test.claim.live_deploy.emit carries an enrolled claim
// asserting exactly that absence. So the re-arm trigger I wrote could never fire: no apply will
// ever install those units. That is the stalled-trigger shape DESIGN section 4b names, a `cannot
// climb` wearing the label `can climb after one event`, and it would have sent a later session
// waiting on an apply that cannot deliver.
//
// WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE: gunbc.live_deploy.emit emit_fleet_converge_service_unit_doc and
// emit_fleet_converge_timer_unit_doc now have no member, no spec consumer, and no oracle. They are
// orphaned emitters, and under delete-first they are the NEXT root to cut rather than evidence to
// re-arm. This is recorded as an observation for that cut, not as an obligation on this one — no
// trigger is declared here, because there is no event that would discharge it.
//
fn live_spec() -> DeploymentSpec {
  deployment_spec_srv1()
}

// The revision the live slot is actually serving, read from its ExecStart on the same date.
// Bound rather than synthesised because it appears verbatim in the emitted unit.
fn live_revision() -> ReleaseRevisionBinding {
  RevisionBoundAtEmission { revision: "b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd" as CommitSha }
}

// gunbc-roadmap-belt.service, as deployed on srv1.
//
// THIS ROW AGREES WITH THE LIVE BYTES AGAIN, and the round trip is worth recording because the
// intermediate state was committed. An earlier cut added EnvironmentFile=-/etc/... here and annotated
// the row as DELIBERATELY DIVERGING until the next apply -- honest at the time, and describing a
// change that was then withdrawn: binding an ambient credential into this multipurpose unit hands the
// token to every child the belt spawns, including the dispatched provider session. See
// live_deploy.spec deployment_credential_env_path for why the withdrawal happened and what replaces
// it.
//
// WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE DEFECT THE OLD ANNOTATION NAMED: publish_pass still refuses on every tick
// with "GITHUB_TOKEN is not set in the belt's environment" (belt-last-tick.json, observed
// 2026-08-27). That refusal is UNCHANGED and is now correct rather than pending -- the belt is not
// supposed to hold that credential. It closes when the publication-only consumer boundary lands, not
// when this unit is next applied.
test fn the_belt_service_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_belt_service_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc roadmap belt launch (queues the one launch obligation: admission, spawn, teardown, discovery)\nAfter=network-online.target\nWants=network-online.target\nOnSuccess=gunbc-roadmap-belt-credential-recovery.service\nOnFailure=gunbc-roadmap-belt-credential-recovery.service\n\n[Service]\nType=oneshot\nTimeoutStartSec=2700s\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-roadmap.slice\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/gunbc/gunbc\nEnvironment=GUNBC_BELT_SPAWN_WORKDIR=/opt/gunbc/gunbc\nEnvironment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\nEnvironment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\nExecStart=/opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_belt_tick_cli.dag --function belt_launch_enqueue_cli\n"
}

// THE WATCHDOG DECISION, PINNED AT DIRECTIVE LEVEL, because the byte row above is an
// emitter-regression pin that a hand-edit can satisfy without the decision being made: the belt
// service must carry a TimeoutStartSec bound (a wedged tick is killed loudly and the cadence
// resumes) and it must be the ONE bound the emitter names, not a second literal. TimeoutStartSec on
// a Type=oneshot unit bounds the whole run; when it fires, the unit fails, the journal records the
// kill, and OnUnitInactiveSec schedules the next tick from that inactivity -- the failure mode the
// 2026-09-21 wedge demonstrated (hours at 100% CPU, timer frozen, receipt absent) becomes one lost
// pass plus a loud failure instead.
test fn the_belt_service_unit_carries_the_per_tick_watchdog_bound() -> Bool {
  service_has(
    file: live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: live_spec()),
    target: TimeoutStartSec { duration: systemd_time_span_of_seconds(d: instance_obligation_run_bound(kind: LaunchObligation)) },
  )
}


// gunbc-roadmap.service, as deployed on srv1. LIKE THE BELT ROW ABOVE, this briefly carried an
// EnvironmentFile line and no longer does, for the same reason: POST /publish/NODE_ID reaches the
// same credential resolver the belt tick does (roadmap_serve
// serve_publish_handler_response_for_instance -> belt_publish_node_for_instance), so crediting THIS
// long-running multipurpose server was the same over-grant in a second place. Both routes will share
// one publication boundary rather than one ambient token; live_deploy.spec
// deployment_credential_env_path carries the argument.
test fn the_serve_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_systemd_unit_doc(spec: live_spec(), revision: live_revision(), now_epoch: claim_oidc_now()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc roadmap HTTP server (gunbc serve)\nAfter=network-online.target tailscaled.service\nWants=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=simple\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-roadmap.slice\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/gunbc/gunbc\nEnvironment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\nEnvironment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\nExecStart=/opt/gunbc/bin/gunbc serve --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_serve.dag --function roadmap_serve_handle --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080 --release-revision b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd --eval-budget-cpu-ms 30000 --eval-budget-wall-ms 120000\nRestart=on-failure\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\n"
}

// gunbc-tree-sync.service, AS EMITTED, and this row now asserts the emission rather than a
// transcription. Its predecessor kept a 2026-08-19 transcription of the deployed bytes carrying a
// SECOND ExecStart -- the `.git` rsync leg -- and named its one intended divergence, the widened
// exclude run on the first leg. The `.git` leg was deleted from the emitter when Git-native
// repository convergence replaced file transport of repository metadata (gunbc.live_deploy.
// repository_convergence_placement), and the expectation was never moved, so this witness was red
// RETIRED WITH THE ROUTE IT PINNED: the_tree_sync_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes_plus_the_exclusion_widening.
//
// It pinned the byte-exact ExecStart of the rsync tree-sync unit. That unit is no longer written:
// the publication member now installs the repository-convergence oneshot and nothing else, so the
// rsync program has no producer and the oracle had no subject. A byte oracle over bytes nobody
// emits is permanently green and would be read as coverage of a route that does not run.
//
// WHAT IS NOT LOST WITH IT. The exclusion list this witness also covered is still asserted, by the
// witnesses over deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes and path_is_deployed -- that authority survives
// because it answers "must not be shipped" for candidate admission, which outlives the rsync that
// used to consume it. Only the ExecStart pin went, because only the ExecStart lost its subject.

// THE SLICE UNIT, BYTE FOR BYTE. The bound is the runner slot's allowance rendered by the
// resource-control authority; a change to either shows up here as the deployed bytes changing.
test fn the_slice_unit_matches_the_deployed_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_slice_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc deployment cgroup bound for gunbc-roadmap.service\n\n[Slice]\nMemoryMax=56908316672\nMemoryHigh=52613349376\nMemorySwapMax=0\n"
}


// THE BROKER UNIT. Every difference from the roadmap unit above is the split: a different
// Description, a different Slice=, a different --entry and --function, a different --port, and NO
// user-manager Environment= lines -- the broker starts no user unit and observes none, so handing
// it the manager's address would be a capability its four routes have no use for.
//
// THE TWO EVALUATION-BUDGET FLAGS ARE PROJECTED, NOT TRANSCRIBED, and the rest of the line is
// pinned. They are a separately owned policy (gunbc.live_deploy.spec dashboard_serve_cpu_limit /
// dashboard_serve_wall_limit) with its own declared basis, so transcribing them here would red this
// oracle for a budget change that has nothing to do with the broker -- and DESIGN section 6 is
// explicit that a measurement is cited by naming its producer rather than by copying its number.
// What this oracle exists to pin is the broker's IDENTITY in the unit: which description, which
// slice, which entry, which function, which port, which principal, and the ABSENCE of the two
// Environment lines. Those are all still exact.
//
// THE BUDGETS BEING THE ROADMAP'S IS ITS OWN CLAIM below, so projecting them here does not lose the
// fact that the broker currently inherits them.
// THE NAME SAYS "WOULD INSTALL", NOT "DEPLOYED", AND THE DISTINCTION IS NOT PEDANTIC. Every other
// oracle in this file was transcribed from a unit read off srv1; this one never was, because the
// broker unit has never run there -- stage A installed it and systemd restart-looped it. Calling it
// "the deployed bytes" would make this the one row in the file whose title asserts an observation
// nobody made, and the neighbouring rows would lend it their credibility.
//
// WHAT IT THEREFORE IS: a pin on the bytes this emitter WOULD install, which is still worth having
// -- it is what makes a silent change to the broker's identity, slice, entry, port or principal
// fail here rather than on the host. What it is not is evidence that a process ever started from
// them. That evidence is the stage-A readback, and until it exists this row should not be cited as
// though it had been taken.
//
// THE RELEASE LOCUS IS IN THESE BYTES NOW (operator ruling 2026-09-20): WorkingDirectory and
// ExecStart both name /opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/<candidate sha>, and so does the
// GUNBC_WORKSPACE_ROOT environment line the seed refuses to run without (live_deploy.emit
// release_locus_workspace_root_environment, since #12011), so the unit starts a
// binary copied from this deploy's verified artifact against sources copied from this deploy's
// checkout. The previous bytes pinned here named the roadmap's root and the roadmap's frozen
// binary, which is the pair of defects the ruling resolved.
test fn the_approval_broker_unit_would_install_these_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_approval_broker_unit_doc(spec: live_spec(), revision: live_revision()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  let budgets = join([
    eval_budget_flag_text(flag: "--eval-budget-cpu-ms", limit: live_spec().approval_broker.serve_cpu_limit),
    eval_budget_flag_text(flag: "--eval-budget-wall-ms", limit: live_spec().approval_broker.serve_wall_limit),
  ], "")
  emitted == join([
    "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc approval broker HTTP server (gunbc serve)\nAfter=network-online.target tailscaled.service gunbc-device-crypto-sidecar.service\nWants=network-online.target gunbc-device-crypto-sidecar.service\n\n[Service]\nType=simple\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-approval-broker.slice\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd\nEnvironmentFile=/opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd/device-crypto-deployed-digest.env\nEnvironment=GUNBC_WORKSPACE_ROOT=/opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd\nEnvironment=GUNBC_APPROVAL_BROKER_BASE_URL=https://srv1.tailecbe08.ts.net\nExecStart=/opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd/gunbc serve --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/auth/approval_broker_serve.dag --function approval_broker_handle --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8085 --release-revision b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd",
    budgets,
    "\nRestart=on-failure\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\n",
  ], "")
}

// THE SIDECAR UNIT: binds the typed port row the broker's client dials, runs the binary installed in the locus, and the
// broker is ordered after it and loads the digest of that same installed binary from its EnvironmentFile.
test fn the_sidecar_unit_would_install_these_bytes() -> Bool {
  let emitted = render(doc: emit_device_crypto_sidecar_unit_doc(spec: live_spec(), revision: live_revision()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  emitted == join([
    "[Unit]\nDescription=gunbc device-crypto verifier sidecar (native serve entry)\nAfter=network-online.target\n\n[Service]\nType=simple\nUser=briansrls\nGroup=briansrls\nSlice=gunbc-approval-broker.slice\nWorkingDirectory=/opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd\nExecStart=/opt/gunbc/approval-broker/releases/b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd/approval_device_crypto_serve --host ",
    approval_device_crypto_sidecar_host as String, " --port ", to_string(approval_device_crypto_sidecar_port as Int),
    " --release-revision b2dd729f92954e50e3d8d9bbd65022c2d58f14dd\nRestart=on-failure\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\n",
  ], "")
}

// THE BROKER INHERITS THE ROADMAP'S EVALUATION CAPS, and that is a stated divergence rather than a
// derivation (gunbc.live_deploy.spec approval_broker_service_config says so): the broker's own
// per-request cost has never been measured, and a cap invented for an unmeasured workload fails as
// a refused approval while a known-safe ceiling fails as a slow one. This claim is what keeps the
// inheritance visible now that the oracle above projects the flags instead of pinning them.
//
// THE RED: give the broker its own caps without saying so, and this fails while the byte oracle
// still passes -- which is exactly the drift projecting the flags would otherwise have hidden.
test fn the_broker_inherits_the_roadmap_evaluation_caps() -> Bool {
  live_spec().approval_broker.serve_cpu_limit == live_spec().service.serve_cpu_limit
    && live_spec().approval_broker.serve_wall_limit == live_spec().service.serve_wall_limit
}

// ── THE DIRECTIVE CLAIMS THE BYTE ORACLES CANNOT MAKE ────────────────────────────────────────
// A byte oracle goes red when anything moves and says nothing about WHY the thing it pinned
// matters. These three relate one directive each to the property the split exists to establish, so
// a future edit that keeps the bytes plausible still has to answer them.

// THE TWO SERVED UNITS DO NOT SHARE A CGROUP. If they did, every byte above could still be right
// and the availability dependency would be back: one process's resident set would count against the
// other's allowance, which is the mechanism, not a side effect.
test fn the_broker_and_the_roadmap_join_different_slices() -> Bool {
  let broker = live_deploy_approval_broker_unit_file(spec: live_spec(), revision: live_revision())
  let roadmap = serve_file()
  service_has(file: broker, target: ServiceSlice { name: gunbc_approval_broker_slice_unit_name })
    && !service_has(file: roadmap, target: ServiceSlice { name: gunbc_approval_broker_slice_unit_name })
    && !service_has(file: broker, target: ServiceSlice { name: deployment_slice_unit_name(names: live_spec().names) })
}

// THE TWO SERVED UNITS DO NOT SHARE A PORT. Two units with two MainPIDs on one socket would be a
// lie the first `ss` exposes, and the front door needs a distinct backend to mount the approval
// paths onto.
test fn the_broker_and_the_roadmap_bind_different_ports() -> Bool {
  !(live_spec().approval_broker.listen.port == live_spec().service.listen.port)
    && live_spec().approval_broker.listen.port == approval_broker_listen_port
}

// THE BROKER'S ALLOWANCE IS ITS OWN AND IS SMALLER. The roadmap's 16 GiB is sized for renders the
// broker never performs; inheriting it would have made the second slice a second name for the first
// bound rather than a bound derived for this process.
test fn the_broker_slice_bound_is_not_the_roadmap_deployment_bound() -> Bool {
  byte_size_count(b: approval_broker_slice_memory_max) < byte_size_count(b: live_deploy_slice_memory_max)
    && slice_has_bytes(file: approval_broker_slice_unit_file(), target: approval_broker_slice_memory_max, as_max: true)
}

// BOTH SERVED SLICES DENY SWAP FROM THE SAME ROW (restored: c11aa2317029 deleted this claim with
// emit_approval_broker_slice_unit_doc, and the typed apply writes approval_broker_slice_unit_file instead).
// The roadmap slice's line is also inside the byte pin above; the broker slice's is pinned nowhere else, and
// at main head d2b027b5f41 deleting it left every surviving claim green. The row's reason is
// gunbc.live_deploy.slice_bounds' own (a swapped page is a stall on the request path), and the broker serves
// the approval path.
test fn both_served_slices_deny_swap_from_the_same_row() -> Bool {
  let expected = join(["MemorySwapMax=", to_string(byte_size_count(b: live_deploy_slice_memory_swap_max))], "")
  string_contains(s: serialize_systemd_unit_file(file: approval_broker_slice_unit_file()), pattern: expected)
    && string_contains(s: render(doc: emit_slice_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol), pattern: expected)
}

// THE CONVERGENCE SLICE CARRIES ITS OWN ROWS, AND FORBIDS SWAP BY ITS OWN ROW. The slice exists so the
// oneshot's bound is sized from its demand rather than inherited; a slice file reading the roadmap
// rows would be the co-tenancy moved into a second file. The swap row is its own because its reason is
// (gunbc.live_deploy.slice_bounds repository_convergence_slice_memory_swap_max), and the rendered text
// is checked so a serializer dropping the line is red as well.
test fn the_convergence_slice_carries_its_own_bounds() -> Bool {
  let file = repository_convergence_slice_unit_file()
  let text = render(doc: emit_repository_convergence_slice_unit_doc(), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  slice_has_bytes(file: file, target: repository_convergence_slice_memory_max, as_max: true)
    && slice_has_bytes(file: file, target: repository_convergence_slice_memory_high, as_max: false)
    && byte_size_count(b: repository_convergence_slice_memory_high) < byte_size_count(b: repository_convergence_slice_memory_max)
    && string_contains(s: text, pattern: join(["MemorySwapMax=", to_string(byte_size_count(b: repository_convergence_slice_memory_swap_max))], ""))
}

test fn the_attempt_worker_slice_carries_the_appropriation_and_forbids_swap() -> Bool {
  match attempt_worker_slice_unit_file() {
    AttemptWorkerSliceUnitFileRefused { cause: _ } => false
    AttemptWorkerSliceUnitFileReady { file } =>
      match attempt_worker_slice_memory_high() {
        UserWorkSliceHighGapExceedsMax => false
        UserWorkSliceHighDerived { bytes: high } =>
          slice_has_bytes(file: file, target: attempt_worker_slice_memory_max, as_max: true)
            && slice_has_bytes(file: file, target: high, as_max: false)
            && string_contains(s: serialize_systemd_unit_file(file: file), pattern: join(["MemorySwapMax=", to_string(byte_size_count(b: live_deploy_slice_memory_swap_max))], ""))
      }
  }
}

test fn the_user_work_slice_carries_pool_derived_bounds_and_forbids_swap() -> Bool {
  match user_work_pool_slots() {
    UserWorkPoolSlotsDerived { slots } => {
      let max = user_work_slice_memory_max_of(slots: slots)
      match user_work_slice_unit_file() {
        UserWorkSliceUnitFileRefused { cause: _ } => false
        UserWorkSliceUnitFileReady { file } =>
          match user_work_slice_memory_high_of(memory_max: max) {
            UserWorkSliceHighGapExceedsMax => false
            UserWorkSliceHighDerived { bytes: high } =>
              slice_has_bytes(file: file, target: max, as_max: true)
                && slice_has_bytes(file: file, target: high, as_max: false)
                && string_contains(s: serialize_systemd_unit_file(file: file), pattern: join(["MemorySwapMax=", to_string(byte_size_count(b: user_work_slice_memory_swap_max))], ""))
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// DIRECTIVE-LEVEL CLAIMS — each relates one directive to the SPEC FIELD it should derive from
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════���══════════════════════════════════════════════
// BELT SERVICE UNIT — directive-level assertions through the typed model
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// DECISIONS ASSERTED:
//   - the belt runs as the deployment's service principal (User=, Group=)
//   - the belt runs inside the deployment's cgroup slice
//   - the belt works in the deployment's service repository tree
//   - the belt carries a user-manager bus environment for the principal's uid
//   - the belt service entrypoint is belt_launch_enqueue_cli (it queues the launch obligation)
//   - the belt spawn workdir environment variable names the repo root

fn belt_service_file() -> SystemdUnitFile {
  live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: live_spec())
}

// THE SECTION ACCESSORS ARE PREDICATES OVER THE COPRODUCT, AND THE WRONG-VARIANT ARM IS `false`,
// NOT AN EMPTY LIST. SystemdUnitFile is closed over three variants; every emitter here returns one
// of them, so an extractor returning a section list needs an arm for the two it does not expect.
// The tempting arm is `_ => []`, and it is the fabricated plausible output DESIGN section 5
// forbids: every membership test over an empty list is false, so an ABSENCE assertion would pass
// VACUOUSLY -- and three of the six REDs below assert absence. That one arm would green the
// evidence while the emitter did anything at all.
//
// `false` in the same position is not a default but the correct answer: if the belt-service
// emitter returned a TimerUnitFile, the claim that the belt unit runs as the service principal IS
// false, and the witness should say so rather than extract nothing and shrug.
//
// unit_has carries no default arm, deliberately. Every variant has a `unit` section, so all three
// arms are real and the match is exhaustive without one; a `_ => false` there would be a dead arm
// asserting the accessor can fail in a way it cannot.

fn service_has(file: SystemdUnitFile, target: SystemdServiceDirective) -> Bool {
  match file {
    ServiceUnitFile { unit: _, service: s, install: _ } => has_directive(dirs: s, target: target)
    _ => false
  }
}

fn unit_has(file: SystemdUnitFile, target: SystemdUnitDirective) -> Bool {
  match file {
    ServiceUnitFile { unit: u, service: _, install: _ } => has_directive_unit(dirs: u, target: target)
    TimerUnitFile { unit: u, timer: _, install: _ } => has_directive_unit(dirs: u, target: target)
    SliceUnitFile { unit: u, slice: _, install: _ } => has_directive_unit(dirs: u, target: target)
    PathUnitFile { unit: u, path: _, install: _ } => has_directive_unit(dirs: u, target: target)
  }
}

fn timer_has(file: SystemdUnitFile, target: SystemdTimerDirective) -> Bool {
  match file {
    TimerUnitFile { unit: _, timer: t, install: _ } => has_directive_timer(dirs: t, target: target)
    _ => false
  }
}

fn slice_has_bytes(file: SystemdUnitFile, target: ByteSize, as_max: Bool) -> Bool {
  match file {
    SliceUnitFile { unit: _, slice: sl, install: _ } =>
      has_slice_directive_val(dirs: sl, target: target, as_max: as_max)
    _ => false
  }
}

// THE EXEC-START COMPARISON REFUSES ON A VARIANT MISMATCH RATHER THAN COMPARING NOTHING. Both
// sides must be service units and both must carry an ExecStart; a comparison that quietly held
// because one side yielded no directives would be a RED that passes while testing nothing, which
// is the failure this file exists to remove.
fn exec_start_changed(baseline: SystemdUnitFile, mutated: SystemdUnitFile) -> Bool {
  match baseline {
    ServiceUnitFile { unit: _, service: b, install: _ } =>
      match mutated {
        ServiceUnitFile { unit: _, service: m, install: _ } =>
          any(b, predicate: fn(d) {
            match d {
              ExecStart { command: c } => !has_directive(dirs: m, target: ExecStart { command: c })
              _ => false
            }
          })
            && any(m, predicate: fn(d) {
              match d {
                ExecStart { command: _ } => true
                _ => false
              }
            })
        _ => false
      }
    _ => false
  }
}

// DECISION: the belt runs as the deployment's service principal.
test fn belt_service_runs_as_service_principal() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: ServiceUser { name: svc.service_user })
    && service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: ServiceGroup { name: svc.service_user })
}

// DECISION: the belt runs inside the deployment's cgroup slice.
test fn belt_service_joins_deployment_slice() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: ServiceSlice { name: deployment_slice_unit_name(names: spec.names) })
}

// DECISION: the belt works in the deployment's repo tree.
test fn belt_service_works_in_repo_tree() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: WorkingDirectory { path: spec.service.repo_root })
}

// DECISION: the belt carries a user-manager bus environment for the principal's uid.
test fn belt_service_carries_user_manager_environment() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  let envs = user_manager_environment_directives(uid: svc.service_uid)
  for_all(envs, predicate: fn(e) { service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: e) })
}

// DECISION: the belt spawn workdir env var names the repo root.
test fn belt_service_carries_spawn_workdir() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: Environment {
    assignment: join([belt_spawn_workdir_env_var as String, "=", svc.repo_root as String], "") as NonEmptyStr,
  })
}

// DECISION: the belt ticks via the declared belt-run-once CLI entrypoint.
test fn belt_service_execstart_is_belt_tick_cli() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: ExecStart { command: join([
    fixed_serve_binary_path(binary: svc.serve_binary),
    " run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2",
    " --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_belt_tick_cli.dag --function belt_launch_enqueue_cli",
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr })
}

// DECISION: the belt unit is Type=oneshot (it runs once per tick and exits).
test fn belt_service_type_is_oneshot() -> Bool {
  service_has(file: belt_service_file(), target: ServiceType { mode: OneshotMode })
}

// DECISION: the belt unit has a Description, After=network-online.target, and Wants=network-online.target.
test fn belt_service_has_unit_directives() -> Bool {
  let f = belt_service_file()
  unit_has(file: f, target: Description { text: "gunbc roadmap belt launch (queues the one launch obligation: admission, spawn, teardown, discovery)" as NonEmptyStr })
    && unit_has(file: f, target: After { target: "network-online.target" as NonEmptyStr })
    && unit_has(file: f, target: Wants { target: "network-online.target" as NonEmptyStr })
}

// DECISION: the belt unit is NOT installable (no [Install] section).
test fn belt_service_is_not_installable() -> Bool {
  match belt_service_file() {
    ServiceUnitFile { unit: _, service: _, install: NotInstallable } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// BELT EVENT PATH UNITS — directive-level assertions through the typed model
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// DECISIONS ASSERTED (dogfood intervention #4: there is no belt timer):
//   - the exit path activates the exit drain and watches the instance's exit inbox as a LEVEL
//   - the publication-answer path activates discovery and watches the answer directory as an EDGE
//   - both are installable under paths.target, and both services run as the belt service does

fn path_has_line(file: SystemdUnitFile, line: String) -> Bool {
  any(systemd_unit_file_lines(file: file), l => l == line)
}

test fn the_exit_path_wakes_the_drain_on_a_non_empty_inbox() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  let f = live_deploy_belt_exit_path_unit_file(spec: spec)
  path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["Unit=", deployment_belt_exit_drain_service_unit_name(names: spec.names) as String], ""))
    && path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["DirectoryNotEmpty=", attempt_exit_inbox_dir(instance_root: spec.service.instance_root as String)], ""))
    && path_has_line(file: f, line: "WantedBy=paths.target")
}

// THE BOOT RECONCILE'S TARGET MATCHES THE MANAGER THE DRAIN IS INSTALLED INTO: a unit under the system unit
// directory is wanted by multi-user.target (the system manager), and the derived target is what the
// emitted drain unit carries -- not a literal. A user-manager install would need default.target.
test fn the_drain_is_wanted_by_the_boot_target_of_the_manager_it_is_installed_into() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  let f = live_deploy_belt_exit_drain_service_unit_file(spec: spec)
  let dest = deployment_belt_exit_drain_service_unit_path(names: spec.names) as String
  let target = deployment_belt_unit_install_target(names: spec.names) as String
  path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["WantedBy=", target], ""))
    && starts_with(s: dest, prefix: "/etc/systemd/system/") && target == "multi-user.target"
}

test fn the_answer_path_wakes_discovery_on_a_changed_answer_directory() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  let f = live_deploy_belt_answer_path_unit_file(spec: spec)
  path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["Unit=", deployment_belt_discovery_service_unit_name(names: spec.names) as String], ""))
    && path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["PathChanged=", publication_answer_dir(instance_root: spec.service.instance_root as String)], ""))
    && path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["PathChanged=", publication_refused_dir(instance_root: spec.service.instance_root as String)], ""))
    && !path_has_line(file: f, line: join(["DirectoryNotEmpty=", publication_answer_dir(instance_root: spec.service.instance_root as String)], ""))
}

test fn the_event_services_run_their_entries_as_the_belt_service_does() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  let drain = systemd_unit_file_lines(file: live_deploy_belt_exit_drain_service_unit_file(spec: spec))
  let discovery = systemd_unit_file_lines(file: live_deploy_belt_discovery_service_unit_file(spec: spec))
  let belt = systemd_unit_file_lines(file: live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: spec))
  let shared = ["Type=oneshot", "User=briansrls", "Slice=gunbc-roadmap.slice", "Environment=GUNBC_BELT_SPAWN_WORKDIR=/opt/gunbc/gunbc"]
  for_all(shared, predicate: fn(l) { any(drain, d => d == l) && any(discovery, d => d == l) && any(belt, d => d == l) })
    && any(drain, l => string_contains(s: l, pattern: "--function belt_exit_drain_cli"))
    && any(discovery, l => string_contains(s: l, pattern: "--function belt_publication_answer_drain_cli"))
}

test fn the_belt_oneshot_starts_credential_recovery_on_success_or_failure() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  let recovery = deployment_belt_credential_recovery_unit_name(names: spec.names) as String
  let belt = systemd_unit_file_lines(file: live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: spec))
  let recover = systemd_unit_file_lines(file: live_deploy_belt_credential_recovery_unit_file(spec: spec))
  any(belt, l => l == join(["OnSuccess=", recovery], ""))
    && any(belt, l => l == join(["OnFailure=", recovery], ""))
    && any(recover, l => string_contains(s: l, pattern: "--function belt_credential_recovery_cli"))
    && !any(recover, l => starts_with(s: l, prefix: "WantedBy="))
}

test fn the_recovery_backstop_timer_starts_the_recovery_unit() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  let recovery = deployment_belt_credential_recovery_unit_name(names: spec.names) as String
  let timer = systemd_unit_file_lines(file: live_deploy_belt_credential_recovery_timer_unit_file(spec: spec))
  any(timer, l => l == join(["Unit=", recovery], ""))
    && any(timer, l => l == join(["OnUnitInactiveSec=", systemd_time_span_of_seconds(d: belt_credential_recovery_backstop) as String], ""))
    && any(timer, l => l == join(["OnBootSec=", systemd_time_span_of_seconds(d: belt_credential_recovery_backstop) as String], ""))
    && any(timer, l => l == "WantedBy=timers.target")
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SERVE UNIT — directive-level assertions through the typed model
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// DECISIONS ASSERTED:
//   - the serve unit runs as the deployment's service principal
//   - the serve unit joins the deployment's cgroup slice
//   - the serve unit works in the deployment's repo tree
//   - the serve unit carries a user-manager bus environment for the principal's uid
//   - the serve entrypoint is serve_handle, on the declared host/port, with the admitted revision
//   - the serve unit restarts on failure
//   - the serve unit is installable as WantedBy=multi-user.target

fn serve_file() -> SystemdUnitFile {
  serve_file_of(spec: live_spec())
}

fn serve_file_of(spec: DeploymentSpec) -> SystemdUnitFile {
  match live_deploy_serve_unit_emission(spec: spec, revision: live_revision(), now_epoch: claim_oidc_now()) {
    LiveDeployServeUnitEmitted { file } => file
    LiveDeployServeUnitOidcReadbackRefused { cause: _ } => live_deploy_slice_unit_file(spec: spec)
  }
}

// DECISION: the serve unit runs as the deployment's service principal.
test fn serve_unit_runs_as_service_principal() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  service_has(file: serve_file(), target: ServiceUser { name: svc.service_user })
    && service_has(file: serve_file(), target: ServiceGroup { name: svc.service_user })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit joins the deployment's cgroup slice.
test fn serve_unit_joins_deployment_slice() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  service_has(file: serve_file(), target: ServiceSlice { name: deployment_slice_unit_name(names: spec.names) })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit works in the deployment's repo tree.
test fn serve_unit_works_in_repo_tree() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  service_has(file: serve_file(), target: WorkingDirectory { path: spec.service.repo_root })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit carries a user-manager bus environment for the principal's uid.
test fn serve_unit_carries_user_manager_environment() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  let envs = user_manager_environment_directives(uid: svc.service_uid)
  for_all(envs, predicate: fn(e) { service_has(file: serve_file(), target: e) })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit is Type=simple (long-running process).
test fn serve_unit_type_is_simple() -> Bool {
  service_has(file: serve_file(), target: ServiceType { mode: SimpleMode })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit restarts on failure.
test fn serve_unit_restarts_on_failure() -> Bool {
  service_has(file: serve_file(), target: Restart { policy: "on-failure" as NonEmptyStr })
}

// DECISION: the serve ExecStart carries the admitted entrypoint, host/port, and revision.
test fn serve_unit_execstart_carries_admitted_entrypoint() -> Bool {
  let svc = live_spec().service
  let port = to_string(svc.listen.port)
  let host = svc.listen.host
  let rev = match live_revision() {
    RevisionBoundAtEmission { revision: r } => r as String
    RevisionNotInstalled => ""
  }
  service_has(file: serve_file(), target: ExecStart { command: join([
    fixed_serve_binary_path(binary: svc.serve_binary),
    " serve --source-root dag --source-root src/v2",
    " --entry dag/gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_serve.dag --function ", svc.serve_function as String,
    " --host ", host, " --port ", port,
    " --release-revision ", rev,
    " --eval-budget-cpu-ms 30000 --eval-budget-wall-ms 120000",
  ], "") as NonEmptyStr })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit has a Description, After=network-online.target tailscaled.service, Wants=network-online.target.
test fn serve_unit_has_unit_directives() -> Bool {
  let f = serve_file()
  unit_has(file: f, target: Description { text: "gunbc roadmap HTTP server (gunbc serve)" as NonEmptyStr })
    && unit_has(file: f, target: After { target: "network-online.target tailscaled.service" as NonEmptyStr })
    && unit_has(file: f, target: Wants { target: "network-online.target" as NonEmptyStr })
}

// DECISION: the serve unit is installable as WantedBy=multi-user.target.
test fn serve_unit_is_installable_as_multi_user_target() -> Bool {
  match serve_file() {
    ServiceUnitFile { unit: _, service: _, install: Installable { directives: ds } } =>
      has_directive_install(dirs: ds, target: WantedBy { target: "multi-user.target" as NonEmptyStr })
    _ => false
  }
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// TREE SYNC UNIT — directive-level assertions through the typed model
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// DECISIONS ASSERTED:
// RETIRED WITH THE RSYNC ROUTE: the tree-sync decision block and its helper.
//
// Seven decisions and their fixture pinned the rsync tree-sync unit -- its principal, slice,
// environment file, ExecStart, Type=oneshot, NotInstallable and description. The publication member
// no longer writes that unit: it installs the repository-convergence oneshot and nothing else, so
// every one of those decisions lost its subject. A decision over a unit no producer emits is
// permanently green and reads as coverage of a route that does not run.
//
// THE PRINCIPAL AND SLICE FACTS ARE NOT LOST. They are decisions about the CONVERGENCE unit now,
// and that unit is covered by repository_convergence_placement's own witnesses, which assert the
// service principal it runs as -- the fact this block was really protecting.


// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// SLICE UNIT — directive-level assertions through the typed model
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════��══════════
//
// DECISIONS ASSERTED:
//   - the slice is derived from the deployment's unit name
//   - the memory bounds come from live_deploy_slice_memory_max / _high (the deployment's own rows)

fn slice_file() -> SystemdUnitFile {
  live_deploy_slice_unit_file(spec: live_spec())
}

// DECISION: the slice's Description names the deployment's unit.
test fn slice_description_names_the_unit() -> Bool {
  let spec = live_spec()
  match slice_file() {
    SliceUnitFile { unit: u, slice: _, install: _ } =>
      has_directive_unit(dirs: u, target: Description {
        text: join(["gunbc deployment cgroup bound for ", spec.names.unit_name as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
      })
    _ => false
  }
}

// DECISION: the slice forbids swap outright. The RED is the 2026-09-18 state -- MemorySwapMax
// unlimited, the serve heap 9.7 GB on disk, requests stalling on swap-in -- which this witness
// distinguishes from any nonzero allowance, not only from absence.
test fn slice_forbids_swap() -> Bool {
  match slice_file() {
    SliceUnitFile { unit: _, slice: dirs, install: _ } =>
      any(dirs, predicate: fn(d) {
        match d {
          SystemdSliceDirective { property: MemorySwapMax, value: DirectiveByteCount { bytes: b } } =>
            byte_size_count(b: b) == 0
          _ => false
        }
      })
    _ => false
  }
}

// DECISION REVERSED 2026-09-12, RESTATED 2026-09-18: the slice's bounds are the DEPLOYMENT'S OWN
// rows, not the runner slot's. The old row asserted derivation from gunbc_runner_slot_desired(); the
// floor-demand ruling falsified that identity. Until 2026-09-18 this witness also pinned the
// decoupling by VALUE INEQUALITY against the runner row -- which is the same coincidence-read-as-law
// in the other direction: the operator sized both envelopes to 26 GiB on the same day for
// independent reasons, and an inequality clause would have refused a correct row. Independence is a
// fact about which declaration the emitter READS (live_deploy_slice_memory_max, asserted here), not
// about whether two numbers happen to differ.
test fn slice_memory_bounds_are_the_deployments_own() -> Bool {
  slice_has_bytes(file: slice_file(), target: live_deploy_slice_memory_max, as_max: true)
    && slice_has_bytes(file: slice_file(), target: live_deploy_slice_memory_high, as_max: false)
}

// DECISION: the slice unit has NotInstallable (no [Install] section).
test fn slice_unit_is_not_installable() -> Bool {
  match slice_file() {
    SliceUnitFile { unit: _, slice: _, install: NotInstallable } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RED (EXECUTED) — each builds a spec with ONE field mutated and shows the positive claim FAIL
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

fn spec_with_principal(wrong_user: NonEmptyStr) -> DeploymentSpec {
  let base = live_spec()
  match base {
    DeploymentSpec { target: t, steps: s, names: n, service: svc, approval_broker: ab, instance_id: iid } =>
      match svc {
        DeploymentServiceConfig { unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_entry: se, serve_function: f,
          provider_state_root: ps,
          instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr, service_uid: uid,
          listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall } =>
          DeploymentSpec {
            target: t, steps: s, names: n, instance_id: iid, approval_broker: ab, directories: base.directories, role_singletons: base.role_singletons,
            service: DeploymentServiceConfig {
              unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_entry: se, serve_function: f,
              provider_state_root: ps,
              instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr,
              service_user: wrong_user,
              service_uid: uid, listen: l,
              serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall,
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE MUTATED VALUES ARE DERIVED FROM THE REAL ONES, NOT TYPED OUT. A hand-written "wrong" value
// is a literal that stops diverging the moment the real value changes to match it, which turns a
// RED silently green -- the same decay #10613 documents for the byte rows one section up.

fn divergent_principal() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["not-", live_spec().service.service_user as String], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn divergent_port() -> Port {
  live_spec().service.listen.port + 1
}

fn divergent_repo_root() -> NonEmptyStr {
  join([live_spec().service.repo_root as String, "-divergent"], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn spec_with_repo_root(wrong_root: NonEmptyStr) -> DeploymentSpec {
  match live_spec() {
    DeploymentSpec { target: t, steps: s, names: n, service: svc, approval_broker: ab, instance_id: iid } =>
      match svc {
        DeploymentServiceConfig { unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_entry: se, serve_function: f,
          provider_state_root: ps,
          instance_root: ir, service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
          listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall } =>
          DeploymentSpec {
            target: t, steps: s, names: n, instance_id: iid, approval_broker: ab, directories: live_spec().directories, role_singletons: live_spec().role_singletons,
            service: DeploymentServiceConfig {
              unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_entry: se, serve_function: f,
              provider_state_root: ps,
              instance_root: ir, repo_root: wrong_root,
              service_user: su, service_uid: uid, listen: l,
              serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall,
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn belt_service_file_of(spec: DeploymentSpec) -> SystemdUnitFile {
  live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: spec)
}

// RED: a spec with a different service_user makes belt_service_runs_as_service_principal go red.
test fn belt_service_user_goes_red_under_different_user() -> Bool {
  let wrong_user = divergent_principal()
  let f = belt_service_file_of(spec: spec_with_principal(wrong_user: wrong_user))
  !service_has(file: f, target: ServiceUser { name: live_spec().service.service_user })
    && service_has(file: f, target: ServiceUser { name: wrong_user })
}

// RED: a spec with a different service_user makes serve_unit_runs_as_service_principal go red.
test fn serve_unit_user_goes_red_under_different_user() -> Bool {
  let wrong_user = divergent_principal()
  let f = serve_file_of(spec: spec_with_principal(wrong_user: wrong_user))
  !service_has(file: f, target: ServiceUser { name: live_spec().service.service_user })
    && service_has(file: f, target: ServiceUser { name: wrong_user })
}


fn spec_with_port(wrong_port: Port) -> DeploymentSpec {
  match live_spec() {
    DeploymentSpec { target: t, steps: s, names: n, service: svc, approval_broker: ab, instance_id: iid } =>
      match svc {
        DeploymentServiceConfig { unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_entry: se, serve_function: f,
          provider_state_root: ps,
          instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr, service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
          listen: l, serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall } =>
          DeploymentSpec {
            target: t, steps: s, names: n, instance_id: iid, approval_broker: ab, directories: live_spec().directories, role_singletons: live_spec().role_singletons,
            service: DeploymentServiceConfig {
              unit_name: u, serve_binary: b, serve_entry: se, serve_function: f,
              provider_state_root: ps,
              instance_root: ir, repo_root: rr,
              service_user: su, service_uid: uid,
              listen: HttpServerListenConfig { host: l.host, port: wrong_port },
              serve_cpu_limit: cpu, serve_wall_limit: wall,
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// RED: the serve unit's ExecStart tracks the listen port.
//
// BOTH SIDES ARE READ BACK FROM EMISSIONS, NEITHER IS TYPED. The serve ExecStart carries the
// binary, the entry, the function, the host, the port, the release revision and two budgets in
// one string; spelling either side out here would bake all seven into the evidence and pin a
// revision that changes on every deploy. Emitting twice and requiring the two commands to DIFFER
// asserts exactly the property -- the port reaches ExecStart -- without asserting anything about
// the other six.
test fn serve_unit_execstart_goes_red_under_different_port() -> Bool {
  exec_start_changed(
    baseline: serve_file_of(spec: live_spec()),
    mutated: serve_file_of(spec: spec_with_port(wrong_port: divergent_port())),
  )
}

// RED: a spec with a different repo_root makes belt_service_works_in_repo_tree go red. The
// emitted WorkingDirectory must follow the spec field rather than a baked-in path.
test fn belt_service_working_directory_goes_red_under_different_root() -> Bool {
  let wrong_root = divergent_repo_root()
  let f = belt_service_file_of(spec: spec_with_repo_root(wrong_root: wrong_root))
  !service_has(file: f, target: WorkingDirectory { path: live_spec().service.repo_root })
    && service_has(file: f, target: WorkingDirectory { path: wrong_root })
}


// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HELPER FUNCTIONS
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// `any` IS IMPORTED, NOT DEFINED. std.algebra already carries it, and a second definition here
// would be the re-invention DESIGN section 2 forbids -- net concepts must not grow. The first cut
// of this file hand-rolled both `any` and `all` over a `[first, ..rest]` match arm, which this
// parser does not admit (src/v2/lens/fn_index_depth_agreement records the same refusal) and which
// produced 194 parse errors before the file ran anywhere. The universal is `for_all`, imported from the same module --
// it is NOT called `all` there, which is how a live call site survived a claim that nothing used
// it: a grep for `all(xs:` missed `all(envs, e => ...)` entirely.

fn has_directive(dirs: List<SystemdServiceDirective>, target: SystemdServiceDirective) -> Bool {
  any(dirs, predicate: fn(d) { d == target })
}

fn has_directive_unit(dirs: List<SystemdUnitDirective>, target: SystemdUnitDirective) -> Bool {
  any(dirs, predicate: fn(d) { d == target })
}

fn has_directive_timer(dirs: List<SystemdTimerDirective>, target: SystemdTimerDirective) -> Bool {
  any(dirs, predicate: fn(d) { d == target })
}

fn has_directive_install(dirs: List<SystemdInstallDirective>, target: SystemdInstallDirective) -> Bool {
  any(dirs, predicate: fn(d) { d == target })
}

fn has_slice_directive_val(dirs: List<SystemdSliceDirective>, target: ByteSize, as_max: Bool) -> Bool {
  any(dirs, predicate: fn(d) {
    match d {
      SystemdSliceDirective { property: MemoryMax, value: DirectiveByteCount { bytes: b } } =>
        as_max && b == target
      SystemdSliceDirective { property: MemoryHigh, value: DirectiveByteCount { bytes: b } } =>
        !as_max && b == target
      _ => false
    }
  })
}

// ── THE TREE-CONVERGENCE ONESHOT MUST NOT SHARE THE BROKER SLICE ────────────────────────────
// THE BRIEF NAMED THIS AND main SUPPLIED THE NUMBER THAT MAKES IT LOAD-BEARING. The split was
// ordered with "the tree-convergence oneshot must not share the broker slice", which read as an
// ordinary separation requirement until the 2026-09-18 receipt in gunbc.live_deploy.slice_bounds
// recorded that oneshot at a 5.2 GB peak beside the serve unit's OOM kill. The broker slice is
// 6 GiB. A convergence run inside it would consume nearly the whole allowance and leave the broker
// to be killed by a process that has nothing to do with approvals -- which is the co-tenancy this
// entire change exists to remove, reintroduced one cgroup down.
//
// IT HOLDS BY CONSTRUCTION TODAY, and the claim exists because construction is not self-asserting:
// the convergence unit names its own slice (gunbc_repository_convergence_slice_unit_name, admitted by
// gunbc.live_deploy.repository_convergence_placement convergence_slice_admission, which refuses every
// serving slice) and the broker unit names its own, so the
// property is true until someone edits either Slice= line. THE RED IS EXACTLY THAT EDIT.
test fn the_convergence_oneshot_does_not_join_the_broker_slice() -> Bool {
  let convergence = convergence_unit_doc(
    spec: live_spec(),
    candidate: deploy_witness_release_revision as String,
    receipt_path: "/opt/gunbc/receipts/repository-convergence.json",
    working_directory: "/opt/gunbc/gunbc",
  )
  string_contains(
    s: convergence,
    pattern: join(["Slice=", gunbc_repository_convergence_slice_unit_name as String], ""),
  )
    && !string_contains(
      s: convergence,
      pattern: join(["Slice=", deployment_slice_unit_name(names: live_spec().names) as String], ""),
    )
    && !string_contains(
      s: convergence,
      pattern: join(["Slice=", gunbc_approval_broker_slice_unit_name as String], ""),
    )
}

// ── THE TREE-REMOTE ONESHOT IS A CONVERGENCE ONESHOT, PLACED LIKE ONE ───────────────────────
// On 2026-10-12 the srv1 production apply of af66aa62 waited behind `systemctl restart
// gunbc-roadmap-tree-remote.service`: the unit named the serving slice with no TimeoutStartSec, the
// serving members held that slice at 55.0 GB against its 52.6 GB MemoryHigh, and the oneshot sat in
// mem_cgroup_handle_over_high for fourteen minutes until the operator's wrapper killed the apply. This
// reads the REAL producer's rendered unit: it joins the admitted convergence slice and never the
// serving one, its start is bounded by the declared convergence bound, and it is the tree-remote unit
// (its ExecStart runs ensure_deployed_tree_remote_wet). RED on af66aa62, whose unit names
// gunbc-roadmap.slice and carries no start bound.
test fn the_tree_remote_oneshot_joins_the_convergence_slice_with_its_start_bound() -> Bool {
  let unit = render(doc: emit_tree_remote_unit_doc(spec: live_spec()), proto: live_deploy_protocol)
  string_contains(s: unit, pattern: join(["Slice=", gunbc_repository_convergence_slice_unit_name as String], ""))
    && !string_contains(s: unit, pattern: join(["Slice=", deployment_slice_unit_name(names: live_spec().names) as String], ""))
    && string_contains(s: unit, pattern: join(["TimeoutStartSec=", to_string(value: second_count(s: convergence_unit_start_timeout))], ""))
    && string_contains(s: unit, pattern: "--function ensure_deployed_tree_remote_wet")
}

// THE REFUSAL ARM IS THE SAME ONE: the tree-remote oneshot placed in the serving slice refuses at the
// placement module, naming itself and the slice, rather than rendering a unit. The positive control is
// the claim above, where the same oneshot over the declared convergence slice renders.
test fn the_tree_remote_oneshot_in_a_serving_slice_refuses() -> Bool {
  let serving = deployment_slice_unit_name(names: live_spec().names)
  match convergence_oneshot_rendering(spec: live_spec(), slice: serving, oneshot: tree_remote_convergence_oneshot(spec: live_spec())) {
    ConvergenceUnitRendered { text: _ } => false
    ConvergenceUnitPlacementRefused { reason: r } =>
      string_contains(s: r, pattern: "tree remote convergence") && string_contains(s: r, pattern: serving as String)
  }
}

// AND THE BROKER IS THE ONLY UNIT IN ITS SLICE. The claim above says one particular neighbour stays
// out; this says the slice has exactly one member among the units this deployment emits, so a unit
// added later cannot join it by default and inherit the approval loop's allowance.
test fn only_the_broker_unit_joins_the_broker_slice() -> Bool {
  let broker = live_deploy_approval_broker_unit_file(spec: live_spec(), revision: live_revision())
  let roadmap = serve_file()
  let belt = live_deploy_belt_service_unit_file(spec: live_spec())
  let broker_slice = ServiceSlice { name: gunbc_approval_broker_slice_unit_name }
  service_has(file: broker, target: broker_slice)
    && !service_has(file: roadmap, target: broker_slice)
    && !service_has(file: belt, target: broker_slice)
}

// THE READBACK'S REQUIREMENT IS THE BROKER'S GATE RESULT, one authority: an open gate carrying a requirement the readback
// would never construct itself (another handler identity, one obligation) is joined verbatim, and a refused gate yields no
// expectation at all, so a deploy cannot validate a requirement other than the one the running broker opens on.
test fn the_readback_expectation_is_the_gates_own_requirement_and_a_refused_gate_has_none() -> Bool {
  let q = DeviceCryptoRequirement { handler_identity: "some-other-handler", required: [] }
  let digest = "abc123" as NonEmptyStr
  (match device_crypto_readback_expectation(gate: DeviceCryptoGateOpen { requirement: q }, deployed_digest: digest) {
    Present { value: e } => (e.requirement.handler_identity as String) == "some-other-handler" && (e.deployed_digest as String) == "abc123"
    Absent => false
  })
    && (match device_crypto_readback_expectation(gate: DeviceCryptoGateRefused { status: 503, reason: "fixture" }, deployed_digest: digest) {
      Present { value: _ } => false
      Absent => true
    })
    && (match device_crypto_readback_expectation(gate: device_crypto_gate(r: approval_device_crypto_realization), deployed_digest: digest) {
      Present { value: _ } => true
      Absent => false
    })
}

